(a) The municipal court judge shall not preside over any ethics complaint for which he has an interest. In such case, the city attorney shall appoint an alternate ethics officer upon disclosure by the judge of his interest.
(b) The municipal court judge shall serve in this capacity without additional compensation. The mayor and council of the city shall provide the courtroom for adjudication of ethics complaints and shall provide the judge with such supplies, equipment and legal support services as may be reasonably necessary for it to perform his duties and responsibilities. The city attorney shall initially respond to legal support issues, but may then designate such other legal counsel as the opinion of the city attorney may be appropriate in consideration of potential conflicts and in keeping with the code of professional responsibility.
(c) The municipal court judge as ethics officer shall have the following duties and powers:
(1) To establish procedures, rules and regulations governing hearing ethics complaints so long as such rules allow the accused due process;
(2) To cause the complaint to be served on the member charged as soon as practicable but in no event later than seven calendar days after receipt of a proper, verified complaint. Service may be by personal service or by certified mail, return receipt requested;
(3) To hold a hearing within 30 days after the receipt of complaint unless all interested parties consent to an alternative date;
(4) To prescribe forms, approved by the city attorney, for the disclosure required in this article and to make available to the public information disclosed as provided in this section;
(5) To receive and hear complaints of violations of the standards required by this article;
(6) To make such investigation and response to a complaint as it deems necessary to determine whether any person has violated any provisions of this article;
(7) To hold such hearings and make such inquiries as deemed necessary to investigate and rule upon complaints;
(8) To report its findings to the mayor and council and make recommendation for appropriate action by the city council. If an elected official is the accused, he shall not participate in the vote or discussion.
(Ord. No. 2016-02, § 1(2-206), 3-21-2016)

